{
  "_comment" : [
    "Two kinds of version, because they answer different questions.",
    "",
    "`version` is the corpus release -- one number for the whole build, which is what a",
    "release note quotes and what the release workflow gates on. Adding data is a minor bump;",
    "changing or removing an existing value is a MAJOR one, because it silently changes every",
    "fixture an existing user has already generated. See docs/corpus-strategy.md.",
    "",
    "`locales` is the same contract per locale, and it exists because the single number was",
    "too blunt to act on. Adding Hindi bumped the corpus for somebody who only uses English,",
    "so their fixtures were flagged as at-risk when nothing they use had moved. A locale's",
    "own version only changes when its own data does.",
    "",
    "`fingerprint` is how that is enforced rather than remembered: the builder hashes each",
    "locale's compiled definitions and refuses to continue if the content moved while the",
    "version did not. `--write-baselines` records the new hashes once the bump is deliberate.",
    "It is the same shape as the NOTICE and locale-module gates -- regenerate, compare, fail",
    "loudly -- applied to the number that people pin."
  ],
